1
00:00:00,467 --> 00:00:02,400
If a microwave heats the food,

2
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:04,333
why is the plate scorching?

3
00:00:04,333 --> 00:00:06,033
Sometimes it's the food:

4
00:00:06,033 --> 00:00:08,167
hot food heats
whatever it touches,

5
00:00:08,167 --> 00:00:10,600
even a dish the waves
pass through.

6
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:13,700
But sometimes the dish
itself absorbs the energy.

7
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:15,467
Most ceramics barely do —

8
00:00:15,467 --> 00:00:17,333
some get very
hot on their own.

9
00:00:17,667 --> 00:00:20,967
So ceramic, glass or plastic
doesn't settle it.

10
00:00:20,967 --> 00:00:22,033
How the dish was made —

11
00:00:22,033 --> 00:00:23,167
and what for — does.

12
00:00:23,467 --> 00:00:26,233
That's what a microwave-safe
label is for.

13
00:00:26,233 --> 00:00:27,300
On plastic,

14
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:29,067
it means the container passed

15
00:00:29,067 --> 00:00:30,967
tests to FDA standards.

16
00:00:30,967 --> 00:00:32,833
Not melting isn't passing.

17
00:00:33,167 --> 00:00:34,267
No label?

18
00:00:34,267 --> 00:00:35,367
Heat the empty dish beside

19
00:00:35,367 --> 00:00:37,067
a cup of water for a minute.

20
00:00:37,067 --> 00:00:39,033
Dish hot? Keep it out.

21
00:00:39,033 --> 00:00:40,600
Dish cool? A good sign —

22
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:42,067
not a guarantee.

23
00:00:42,433 --> 00:00:43,533
Check the label,

24
00:00:43,533 --> 00:00:45,533
follow the maker's
instructions —

25
00:00:45,533 --> 00:00:47,833
and the food should be
the hottest thing out.
